openapi: 3.1.0
info:
  title: FuzeFront Portal API
  version: 1.4.0
  description: >-
    **⚠️ SUPERSEDED (FF-EPIC-17-S7).** The standalone-`portals`-table model this
    contract froze — including the master-admin CRUD (`/api/v1/admin/portals`)
    and its generated `@fuzefront/portal-client` — is SUPERSEDED by portal CRUD
    expressed as ORG-TREE operations in the Security API
    (`packages/security/openapi.yaml`, tag `portals`, `@fuzefront/security-client`
    ≥ 0.7.0). A "portal" is now an `organizations` row whose `parentOrgId` is the
    platform root, carrying a portal-root + tenant attributes — NOT a separate
    `portals` entity. New work MUST target the org-tree endpoints. This file is
    retained for reference during the backend migration and for the still-live
    surfaces (`GET /api/v1/portal/context`, `GET /api/v1/portal/current`) until
    those are reconciled too; `@fuzefront/portal-client` should be **retired**
    once the fan-out moves off it. Retirement, the tenant-attribute storage
    decision (recommended: an `organizations`-keyed extension table), and the
    `portals.id → organizations.id` FK-retargets (FF-EPIC-11/12/13/15/16) are an
    owner/orchestrator decision + migration follow-up, tracked in the
    FF-EPIC-17-S7 contract PR — not executed here.


    REST API for **multi-tenant portals** — the first-class `portals` object that
    lets one shared FuzeFront deployment serve many isolated white-label tenant
    portals. This contract freezes two surfaces:

      1. **Public portal context** (`GET /api/v1/portal/context`) — the
         unauthenticated boot endpoint the shell/login call to resolve the active
         portal (id, slug, branding, identity policy, auth entry points) for the
         current Host. Renders *before* login (a branded login screen), so it
         exposes only public branding/identity-policy/auth-entry data — never a
         raw `portals` row. Source: FF-EPIC-10-S2.

      2. **Master-admin portal CRUD** (`/api/v1/admin/portals`) — create / list /
         get / update / suspend / resume, Permit **platform-admin** gated and
         fail-closed (non-admin ⇒ 403). This surfaces the resumable provisioning
         pipeline behind a versioned, generated client. Source: FF-EPIC-09-S3.

    A portal's own self-scoped identity (`GET /api/v1/portal/current`) is also
    frozen here so the portal-admin console can read the caller's own portal
    (resolved from the session, never from a client-supplied id).

    This file is the single source of truth for `@fuzefront/portal-client`.
    Changing the API means amending this file first (see the `api-contract-first`
    skill), bumping `info.version` + the changelog, re-linting (Spectral), then
    regenerating the client (`openapi-typescript`). UI, backend, and tests import
    the SAME generated types, so drift becomes a compile error.

    ### Fail-closed semantics (contract, not decoration)
      * A **suspended** portal fails closed — `403 PORTAL_SUSPENDED`.
      * The **root** portal (slug `fuzefront`) cannot be suspended —
        `409 ROOT_PORTAL_PROTECTED`.
      * Creating a portal with a duplicate slug — `409 SLUG_TAKEN`.
      * A non-platform-admin caller on any admin route — `403 FORBIDDEN`
        (an authorization denial, NOT a sign-in redirect; only `401`
        re-authenticates).
      * A caller acting on a portal they do not administer — `403 FORBIDDEN_PORTAL`,
        with zero cross-tenant data leaked.

    All portal capability is staged behind the master feature flag
    `fuzefront.platform.multi-tenant-portals` (default OFF, FF-EPIC-09-S4); authz
    stays in Permit — the flag is rollout convenience only.

    The master-admin fleet list's `identityMode`/`launchUrl` fields (Portals
    Directory, backend slice S1) are separately staged behind
    `fuzefront.platform.portals-directory` (default OFF) — when OFF,
    `GET /api/v1/admin/portals` omits both fields entirely (not `null`).
    Backend slice S5 adds the read-vs-manage refinement on the SAME flag:
    when ON, the blanket admin-only gate on `GET /api/v1/admin/portals` is
    replaced by a per-portal Permit `read`/`manage` check (`canManage`,
    `canOpen` — both OPTIONAL, same gating), and `launchUrl` is only present
    on rows the caller may launch (`canOpen: true`). When OFF, S5 changes
    nothing — the route stays admin-role gated exactly as before.
  license:
    name: UNLICENSED

